      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for the careful work here -- the whole-file formatter in the parent is a solid core, and the changed-line scoping is clearly well thought through. It even matches JGit\u0027s current Eclipse setting (`format_source_code_changes_only\u003dtrue`), so it\u0027s faithful to how the project formats today.\n\nBefore we settle on the approach, I\u0027d like to raise one project-direction question, since it\u0027s really a trade-off with no wrong answer.\n\nWhat I\u0027m really after is to harmonize JGit\u0027s formatting with gerrit\u0027s: the same `tools/gjf.sh run` workflow, which we could call `tools/jf.sh`:\n\n```\ngit show --diff-filter\u003dAM --name-only HEAD | grep \u0027java$\u0027 | xargs \u003cformatter\u003e\n```\n\nIt lists the files changed in the commit and reformats each one whole -- no changed-line scoping. Which formatter runs behind it (Eclipse JDT here, google-java-format in gerrit) is an implementation detail; the workflow and the developer experience are identical.\n\nJustification:\n\n- Harmonize across projects: gerrit and JGit are developed by largely the same group, who already know and use gerrit\u0027s `tools/gjf.sh run`. A matching `tools/jf.sh run` in JGit gives one consistent formatting workflow across both -- same command, same mental model.\n\n- Clean separation: changed-line scoping keeps diffs minimal, but it formats only the lines a change touches, so over time a file accumulates well-formatted edits while the rest stays as it was. The gerrit model keeps formatting and content strictly separate instead -- a whole-file reformat lands in its own prerequisite change (never mixed with content edits), and the content change follows on the clean file. With a one-time tree-wide format and a CI check to hold it, the tree converges to a consistent state.\n\n@matthias.sohn@sap.com which direction would you prefer -- the self-contained changed-line scoping here, or the gerrit model (a `tools/jf.sh run` doing whole-file formatting of changed files, plus a one-time tree-wide format and CI enforcement)?\n\n(Either way, one small prerequisite -- separate thread on the parent: the CLI would need to apply the project\u0027s `remove_trailing_whitespaces` save action, or a tree-wide format leaves trailing whitespace that Eclipse-on-save strips.)",
